  {"question": "According to arXiv 2507.23718, how does the prevalence of Socioeconomic & Environmental Risks in AI news coverage differ between South Africa and the U.S., and what statistical test supports this?", "answer": "South Africa has the highest proportion (44.6%) and the U.S. a lower proportion (37.3%) of articles covering Socioeconomic & Environmental Risks; a Chi-square test confirms significant differences.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "the highest proportions observed in South Africa (44.6%), United States (37.3%), United Kingdom (36.8%), and Australia (34.5%)"}, {"page": 5, "quote": "A Chi-Square test for this risk category was conducted on the number of articles covering this risk yielding a statistically significant result (χ2 (5) = 54.76, p <0.001), indicating that the distribution of articles discussing socio-economic and environmental risks is not independent of country."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2507.23718, how do right-biased and left-biased U.S. news outlets differ in their coverage of Discrimination & Toxicity AI risks, and what language characterizes their reporting?", "answer": "Right-biased outlets focus on risks like AI 'skewed against conservatives', while left-biased outlets highlight hate speech and discrimination against people of color.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "right-bias outlets mentioned risks pertaining to the false portrayal of \"the founding fathers as people of color\" by Gemini AI and how AI systems like ChatGPT \"trained using Wikipedia, may produce responses skewed against conservatives and in favor of leftists\"."}, {"page": 7, "quote": "left-bias media outlets reported on risks about the \"inclusion of far-right and non-reputable sources\" in AI training data which leads to the \"dissemination of hate speech through AI responses\". Furthermore, risks covered by left-bias outlets also focus on reporting the negative impacts of AI technologies experienced by people of color such as \"financial discrimination\" as a result of \"mortgage approval algorithms discriminat[ing] against applicants of color\", or the wrongful arrest of \"eight month pregnant Porscha Woodruff\" due an AI facial recognition tool flagging Porscha's face as a \"carjacking suspect\"."}]},

  {"question": "According to arXiv 2507.23718, what is the definition of Human-computer interaction risks in the MIT AI Risk Repository taxonomy?", "answer": "Human-computer interaction risks are defined as Overreliance and unsafe use and Loss of human agency and autonomy.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "For instance, Human-computer interaction risks are defined in the domain taxonomy in terms of Overreliance and unsafe use and Loss of human agency and autonomy risks, which were used in prompt A.9."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2507.23718, what is the macro-averaged F1-score achieved by GPT-4o for classifying AI risk impacts from news media?", "answer": "GPT-4o achieved a macro-averaged F1-score of 0.90 for classifying AI risk impacts.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "the performance evaluation of the LLM for classifying impacts from news media into the seven categories of risks from the domain taxonomy of AI risks demonstrates a strong performance by GPT-4o in classifying impacts into their corresponding risk category with a macro-averaged F1-score of 0.90 2 ."}]},

  {"question": "In arXiv 2507.23718, which AI risk category had the lowest average news coverage across all six countries, and which country led in its coverage?", "answer": "Human-Computer Interaction Risks had the lowest average coverage (9.3%), with the U.K. leading at 10.8%.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "As for the Human-Computer Interaction Risks, which reflect risks related to over-reliance, unsafe use, and loss of human agency, it received the least news coverage of AI risks across all countries (9.3%). On average, 8.3% of the news coverage in each country focused on reporting human-computer interaction risks, with the U.K. leading this coverage (10.8%)."}]},

  {"question": "According to arXiv 2507.23718, which statistical test was used to assess the association between AI risk coverage and political bias of U.S. news outlets?", "answer": "A Chi-square test of independence was used to assess the association.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "To statistically test the association between the coverage of various AI risks and domain-bias categories in U.S.-based outlets, we conducted a Chi-square test of independence on the number of articles reporting each risk category for each bias category."}]}
]}
